# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/002_read_write.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_read_write
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Read and write of every bitfield across mixed signedness and width, folded and volatile runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The default undefined-behaviour warning gate applies unmodified, with no deviation:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror.  The program
was verified to pass that gate and to run cleanly under
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all.

Every store lands inside the destination field's representable range.  The folded variant
stores only constants that fit.  The runtime variant reads each field through volatile
storage, adds one, and stores the result: each unsigned destination is masked to its exact
field width, so the conversion back into the field is provably value-preserving, and each
signed destination receives a value one step inside its range.  No signed overflow can occur
because every signed operand is promoted to int before the addition and the result is far
from the int limits.  No shift is performed anywhere in the program.  No aliasing violation:
no pointer is formed to any bitfield and no type-punning of any kind occurs.  No
uninitialized read: the folded object has an explicit constant initializer, and the volatile
object has static storage duration and so is zero-initialized, and in any case every one of
its fields is written before it is read.  No object is modified twice between sequence points;
each read-modify-write is a separate full statement.  No pointer value, address, timestamp,
random value or locale-dependent format is printed.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every bitfield is declared with an explicit signed int or unsigned int base type.  A plain
int bitfield's signedness is implementation-defined, so using it would manufacture a spurious
divergence; stating the signedness in the declaration removes that dependency entirely.  The
struct deliberately mixes both signednesses and five widths (3, 5, 9, 12 and 1 bits) inside a
single four-byte storage unit.

Plain char is not used.  Plain char signedness was measured to differ across the four targets
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64), so it is avoided entirely.

No value whose width varies by target is printed.  sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) differ on
i686 (4) from the other three targets (8), so neither is printed, and no pointer value or
address appears in the output.  Every bitfield is declared on an int base type, and int is
four bytes on all four targets, so every printed value is target-invariant by derivation.
Every printf argument is explicitly cast to unsigned or int, so no default argument promotion
is relied upon implicitly.

All four targets and all three oracles remain enabled.  Bitfield read-back values and the
minimum value of a narrow signed field were measured identical on all four targets, so a
cross-backend divergence here is a genuine finding rather than an implementation-defined
difference.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
folded_read u3=6 s5=-13 u9=400 s12=-1500 u1=1
folded_write u3=1 s5=15 u9=511 s12=2047 u1=0
folded_min s5=-16 s12=-2048
runtime_read u3=6 s5=-13 u9=400 s12=-1500 u1=1
runtime_write u3=7 s5=-12 u9=401 s12=-1499 u1=0
runtime_max u3=7 u9=511 u1=1
runtime_zero u3=0 u9=0 u1=0
END
